CH = dict(num=6, tag="History", theme="Vedic polity, janapadas to empires, administration, society, education, economy",
 title="State and Society up to 1000 CE",
 intro="This chapter follows the Indian subcontinent from clan-based Vedic society to janapadas, mahājanapadas and the great empires of the Mauryas, Guptas and Cholas. It looks at kingship, administration, ethics (dharma), varṇa and jāti, women, religion, education, agriculture, trade and guilds, and shows how deep continuity runs through all of them.",
 big_questions=["How did society and state organise themselves before 1000 CE?", "How did states and societies emerge and spread across the subcontinent?", "How did dharma and the chakravarti samrāṭ ideal create unity in values and governance?", "How did social, administrative and occupational groups take shape and come together culturally?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Understanding Early Indian State and Society"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "A **society** is a system of social relationships among people who share territory, culture and a sense of belonging; it has families, institutions such as marriage, and is regulated mainly by **custom**. A **State** is an organised political system based on rules and laws, with rights and duties of rulers and subjects, governance mechanisms and institutions for law and order."),
 ("p", "From at least the 2nd millennium BCE, literary sources (the **Ṛigveda** first) supplement archaeology, giving a fuller picture of society, politics and ideas. The sweep of the chapter: kin-based polities → janapadas and mahājanapadas → empires (Mauryas, Guptas, Cholas), with continuity in the ideal of a pan-Indian monarch (**aśhvamedha yajña**, **chakravarti samrāṭ**) and in **dharma**."),
 ("table", ["Period", "Development"], [["About 2000 BCE", "Kin-based polities of the Ṛigveda (Sapta-Sindhu region)"], ["1000–600 BCE", "Janapadas; Iron age; PGW culture"], ["600–300 BCE", "Sixteen mahājanapadas; Buddha and Mahāvīra; NBPW"], ["321 BCE", "Mauryan Empire founded by Chandragupta Maurya"], ["272–232 BCE", "Aśhoka; philosophy of Dhamma"], ["185 BCE", "Śhunga empire founded by Puṣhyamitra"], ["300 BCE–300 CE", "Sangam literature; early kingdoms of Tamilakam"], ["78 CE / 30–375 CE", "Saka Era begins / Kuṣhāṇas"], ["320–550 CE", "Guptas"], ["543 CE", "Chālukyas founded by Pulakeśhin I"], ["606 CE", "Harṣhavardhana annexes Kannauj"], ["712 CE", "Arab conquest of Sindh"], ["750–1161 / 753–982 / 730–1036", "Pālas / Rāṣhṭrakūṭas / Gurjara-Pratihāras"], ["985–1014 CE", "Rājarāja I; Brihadeeshwara Temple, Thanjavur"]]),
 ("h2", "The Vedic Period"),
 ("h3", "Vedic literature"),
 ("ul", ["The Vedas are the earliest corpus of Indian literature (the Harappan script is undeciphered). They were oral for centuries. The date of the Ṛigveda is debated (5th to 2nd millennium BCE); it was composed in the north-west (**Sapta-Sindhu**: Indus, its five tributaries and the Sarasvatī), where the Sindhu–Sarasvatī civilisation flourished; both knew copper."]),
 ("table", ["Veda", "Content"], [["**Ṛigveda**", "Earliest; 1,028 hymns (sūktas) praising deities and reflecting on creation, birth and death"], ["**Yajurveda**", "Performative aspects of yajñas; prose explanations"], ["**Sāmaveda**", "Hymns from the Ṛigveda arranged for musical recitation; basis of the seven svaras"], ["**Atharvaveda**", "Wide range of hymns, some against evil; treatment of physical and mental ailments"]]),
 ("p", "Each Veda has four parts: **samhitā** (hymns), **brāhmaṇa** (prose explanation of rituals), **āraṇyaka** (philosophical speculations of forest sages) and **upaniṣhad** (the Self, Ātman, and Ultimate Being, Brāhman). Brāhman is a concept; Brāhmaṇa is a text and also a varṇa."),
 ("h3", "Political institutions"),
 ("ul", ["Early Vedic society was organised into **janas** (kin-based clans); the Ṛigveda mentions about 30, five of them (Yadu, Turvaśha, Puru, Anu, Druhyu) being the **pañchajana**. 'Bharata' first appears for the **Bharata jana**.", "The **rājā** was a clan chief, leading in war and protecting the clan."]),
 ("table", ["Assembly", "Nature"], [["**Sabhā**", "Smaller body of select elites; policy and judicial functions"], ["**Samiti**", "Larger assembly of community members; broader political matters, representing the population"], ["**Vidhata**", "Popular gathering; discussions on warfare and other matters"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "These assemblies checked the rājā, suggesting an early principle of people's participation in governance."),
 ("h2", "Early Kingdoms and Republics"),
 ("ul", ["**Janapada** ('where a people first set its foot'): the shift from kinship to territorial identity, between about 1000 and 600 BCE. Control of land, farming and trade routes grew in importance; the Ganga plains replaced the north-west as the cultural centre.", "Archaeology of the first millennium BCE: more settlements in the Ganga region, iron tools, more grains (especially paddy), **PGW** (Painted Grey Ware) pottery and in the middle Ganga (Prayagraj to eastern Bihar) the glossy 'deluxe' **NBPW** (Northern Black Polished Ware).", "**Mahājanapadas** (about 600 BCE–300 CE): bigger units; sixteen are listed (Kamboja, Gandhāra, Matsya, Kuru, Śhūrasena, Pañchāla, Vatsa, Kosala, Kāśhī, Malla, Vṛijji, Magadha, Anga, Avanti, Chedi, Aśhmaka). **Magadha** (Bihar) became the most powerful thanks to location, fertile plains and strong rulers, leading to the **Mauryan Empire**.", "Forms of government: **monarchical** states (**rājyas**) and **republics** (**gaṇas / saṁghas**).", "South: the **Satavāhanas** (Deccan, 2nd century BCE–3rd century CE, nearly 500 years). Further south: **Cholas** (lower Kaveri), **Pandyas** (Tamraparni and Vaigai), **Keralaputras / Cheras**, and **Satiyaputras**, all in Aśhokan inscriptions."]),
 ("table", ["Early Tamil kingdom", "Emblem", "Region"], [["Chola", "Tiger", "Fertile rice lands of the Kaveri"], ["Chera", "Bow", "Kerala"], ["Pandya", "Fish", "Tamraparni and Vaigai valleys"]]),
 ("p", "Known from **Sangam literature** (300 BCE–300 CE); the three rulers were the 'crowned kings' (**Vendar**). A Chera king earned the title **adhirāja**. These early kingdoms should not be confused with the later imperial ones."),
 ("h2", "Duties and Ideals of the King"),
 ("ul", ["Titles: rājā, mahārāja, samrāṭ. The Arthaśhāstra: 'Only if a king is himself energetically active, do his officers follow him energetically.' The Yajurveda coronation oath: judge the strong and weak fairly, protect the country, do good to the people. The Śhānti Parva of the Mahābhārata guides rulers on ethics and justice.", "The king protected subjects from external threats and internal disorder and dispensed justice (abduction, robbery, theft, adultery); severe punishments were prescribed for grave offences. Kingship was generally hereditary, but kings could also be elected or expelled, so power was not absolute.", "**Pan-Indian awareness:** terms **Jambudvīpa, Bhāratavarṣha, Aśhvamedha and Rājasūya yajña, Prithivi, chakravarti kśhetra**. Aśhoka's edict speaks of changes in the spiritual life of Jambudvīpa; the Arthaśhāstra defines **prithivi** as the area between the Himalayas and the sea = the domain of a universal ruler. The Chera king **Nedunjeral Adan** reached the Himalayas; **Rajendra I** (11th century CE) took the title **Gangaikonda** after conquering the Ganga region."]),
 ("h3", "Council of ministers and the Saptāṅga"),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Saptāṅga:** Kauṭilya's seven constituents of the state: **king (swāmi), ministers (amātya), territory and population (janapada), forts (durga), treasury (koṣha), army/force (daṇḍa), allies (mitra)**. The state works only when all are integrated. 'One wheel does not move the carriage.'"),
 ("ul", ["The **mantri-pariṣhad** was a small body of elder statesmen advising the king; it generally included the treasurer, chief tax collector, chief legal adviser and commander-in-chief. An Aśhokan inscription shows it could decide in the emperor's absence.", "The **Junagadh Rock Inscription** (near Girnar, Gujarat) bears records of three rulers over about 700 years: **Aśhoka** (Major Rock Edicts in Prakrit, Dhamma), **Rudradaman I** (c. 150 CE; Sanskrit; military achievements and repair of the **Sudarshana Lake**) and **Skandagupta** (5th century CE; restoration of the lake)."]),
 ("h2", "How Were Empires Administered?"),
 ("table", ["Level", "North", "South"], [["Kingdom (rājya)", "Divided into provinces: **bhukti**", "**Maṇḍala / maṇḍalam**"], ["Division", "**Viṣhaya / bhoga**", "**Koṭṭam / valanāḍu**"], ["District", "**Adhiṣhṭhāna / paṭṭana**", "**Nāḍu**"], ["Group of villages (tahsil)", "**Vithi**", "**Paṭṭalā / kūṛram**"], ["Village", "Lowest unit; headman **grāmika**", "Village"]]),
 ("ul", ["Satavāhana empire: **āḥāras** (divisions) with **amātyas**. District governors (**pradeśhikas**) did judicial and administrative work, consulting bankers, caravan leaders, artisans and scribes. Cities had governors for revenue and order. About 300–800 CE saw decentralisation.", "**Guptas** kept the old system: mantri heads civil administration; commander-in-chief, general and palace guards chief; new **sāndhivigrahika** (minister of peace and war); **kumārāmātyas** at provincial/local level. The **Damodarpur copper plates** (Kumaragupta I) show a district office of five: head district officer, chief banker, chief caravan trader, chief artisan, chief revenue collector.", "**Pallavas** (c. 275–897 CE): centralised monarchy with local governance; tax-free **Brahmadeya** villages; village assemblies with **variyams** (committees for irrigation, gardens, temples). **Chālukyas** (c. 543–753 CE, Badami): similar; **agrahārams** (Brahmin settlements) at Aihole and Badami became centres of learning.", "**Gurjara-Pratihāras, Pālas and Rāṣhṭrakūṭas** fought the **tri-partite struggle** for **Kannauj**; their administration mixed monarchy with provinces, districts and largely self-reliant villages.", "**Imperial Cholas** (9th–11th century CE): maṇḍalams → valanāḍus → nāḍus → Urs (villages). Village assemblies handled disputes, revenue, land, irrigation, roads and records."]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Uttaramerur inscription** of Parantaka I (10th century CE, Kanchipuram district): describes village elections by the **Kudavolai** ('ballot pot') system. Names of eligible candidates were written on palm leaves in a pot and a young child drew them in public (often at a temple). Winners formed **variyam** committees (tank/irrigation, justice, taxes). Candidates needed 'honest earnings' and 'pure' mind."),
 ("h2", "Ethics: The Foundation of Law and Life"),
 ("ul", ["Vedas and epics discuss right conduct and the universe. The Bhagavad Gītā stresses knowledge, faith, action and virtue.", "**Samatva** (sameness): all bodies are of the same matter and all consciousness is a manifestation of one supreme consciousness (satya, ṛita). The Mahābhārata repeatedly upholds it across varṇas.", "**Ṛita:** all-pervading cosmic order giving harmony and balance in nature and society (Ṛigveda).", "**Dharma** (not religion): duty, obligation, righteousness, moral conduct, from root *dhri* (to uphold). Bhishma: 'whatever upholds all beings is dharma'; 'the dharma of iron is to sink and of wood to float'. Pāli equivalent: **dhamma**. Aśhoka's edicts promoted dhamma: moral conduct, family respect, non-violence, compassion. Works like Kāmandaka's **Nītisāra** and Bāṇabhaṭṭa's **Kādambarī** (one of the first novels) link ethics and kingship."]),
 ("h2", "Social Structures and Everyday Life"),
 ("h3", "Varṇa and jāti"),
 ("table", ["Varṇa", "Expected role"], [["Brāhmaṇa", "Study and teach Vedas, perform yajñas, give and receive dāna"], ["Rajanya (Kṣhatriya)", "Warfare, protection, justice; also study, sponsor yajñas, donate"], ["Vaiśhya", "Agriculture, pastoralism, trade; also study, rituals, charity"], ["Śhūdra", "Assist others; in practice farming, herding, trade, crafts"]]),
 ("ul", ["Earliest mention: **Puruṣhasūkta**, Ṛigveda Book 10. Early Vedic identity depended on many factors (ethnicity, region, village, **gotra**, language, occupation); occupations were flexible. Ṛigveda 9.112.3: 'I am a poet; my father is a physician, my mother grinds corn' shows occupational diversity within a family.", "Varṇa was functional: knowledge ranked highest, then power and wealth. Sutta Nipāta: 'No brāhmaṇa is such by birth but only by his deeds.'", "**Jāti** emerged later through intermarriage, migrating groups becoming **endogamous** (marrying within the group) and territorial differences. Varṇas stayed four; jātis kept multiplying."]),
 ("p", "**Social mobility:** rulers came from varied backgrounds (Nandas, Mauryas, Śhuṅgas, Satavāhanas, Vākāṭakas, Guptas, Puṣhyabhūtis). The **Mandsaur inscription** (473 CE) records silk weavers' guild moving from Lata (Gujarat) to Dashapura (Madhya Pradesh), skilled also in archery and astrology. **Karitalai plates** (Jayanātha, late 5th century) show brāhmaṇas as land managers. Sangam works (Tolkappiyam: Arasar, Vanigar, Velar, Antanar; Pattinappalai) show an occupation-based society."),
 ("h3", "Family, āśhramas and puruṣhārthas"),
 ("ul", ["Smallest unit: **kula** (family) → **grāma** (village) → **viśha** (headed by viśhapati) → **jana** (rājā). Society was **patrilineal**. **Gotra** = patrilineal clan traced to a Vedic ṛiṣhi, guiding marriage.", "Four **āśhramas**: brahmacharya (student), gṛihastha (householder), vānaprastha (forest life), saṁnyāsa (renunciation). Life rites: sixteen **saṁskāras**.", "Four **puruṣhārthas**: **dharma** (righteousness), **artha** (material well-being), **kāma** (desire), **mokṣha** (liberation). Wealth is good when guided by dharma."]),
 ("h3", "Role of women"),
 ("ul", ["Vedic women studied, performed rituals, joined chariot races and the sabhā. Women sages: **Apālā, Viśhvavārā, Ghoṣhā, Lopāmudrā**. Goddesses Uṣha (dawn) and Aditi. Manu-smṛiti 3.56: 'Where women are honoured, there gods rejoice.' Their status later fluctuated.", "Gupta–Vākāṭaka literature shows educated women; **Prabhāvatī Gupta** (daughter of Chandragupta II) ruled as regent in the Vākāṭaka kingdom and issued land grants. Women appear as donors on votive inscriptions.", "Sangam women farmed, herded, spun, fished, made salt and sold garlands; poetesses **Avvaiyar** and **Vennikuyattiyar**. Chola queen **Sembiyan Mahādevī** supported temples."]),
 ("h3", "Religious life and Bhakti"),
 ("ul", ["Vedic gods were forces of nature (sun, rain, fire, dawn) invoked by prayers and **yajñas** (offerings to sacred fire); nature seen as sacred (echoes today in Chhath and Makar Sankranti).", "From the middle of the first millennium BCE came **renunciants** (parivrājaka, bhikṣhu, śhramaṇa); Buddha and Mahāvīra. Vedic worship moved to personal deities Viṣhṇu, Śhiva, Śhakti.", "**Bhakti:** direct devotion open to all regardless of class or gender; prominent from the 6th century in Tamil lands through the **12 Āḻvārs** (Viṣhṇu) and **63 Nāyanmārs** (Śhiva)."]),
 ("h2", "The Quest for Knowledge"),
 ("ul", ["Education cultivated truth, patience, humility, self-control and respect for all beings and trained pupils to live by dharma. Subjects: Vedas, grammar, logic, philosophy, ethics, mathematics, science, medicine, astronomy; also music, dance, painting, archery, yoga and meditation.", "**Guru-śhiṣhya paramparā:** the sacred teacher–pupil bond; the **gurukula** at the teacher's home, with the pupil treated as family; disciplined life.", "Higher-learning centres: **Takṣhaśhilā** (planned campus), **Nālandā** (university, 427 CE), **Vikramśhilā** (783–820 CE), **Vallabhī** (480 CE), Odantapurī, Jagaddalā, Vārāṇasī, Kānchīpuram, Śhāradā Pīṭha and others."]),
 ("table", ["Field", "Examples"], [["Grammar", "Pāṇini's Aṣhṭādhyāyī, Piṅgala's Chhandaśhāstra, Patañjali's Mahābhāṣhya"], ["Law and ethics", "Manu-smṛiti, Yājñavalkya, Nārada, Viṣhṇu Smṛiti"], ["Medicine", "Charakasamhitā, Suśhrutasamhitā"], ["Sanskrit poetry", "Kālidāsa: Raghuvaṃśha, Kumārasambhava"], ["Tamil", "Tirukkural, Silappadikāram, Maṇimēkalai, Sangam texts (akam = love, puram = war/public life)"]]),
 ("h2", "Economy"),
 ("h3", "Agriculture, land revenue and irrigation"),
 ("ul", ["Mauryan state regulated the economy: village land in individual holdings plus common pastures and groves; forests cleared for farming but some protected. Basic land tax: about **one-sixth of produce**. Kauṭilya classified land (cultivated, fallow, high and dry, sown, groves). Crops: rice, pulses, wheat, linseed, mustard, saffron, sugarcane, fruits, vegetables.", "Post-Mauryan: **Milindapañho** (eight stages of farming); cotton on Deccan black soil; **Amarakoṣha** chapter on plants and manure; Sangam texts on Chera jackfruit, pepper, turmeric, ragi and sugarcane.", "**Irrigation:** Puṣhyagupta, Chandragupta Maurya's governor, built a dam on **Sudarshana Lake** (Junagadh record). The Cholas built canals, lakes and tanks; Karikala's **Grand Anicut (Kallanai)** is still used."]),
 ("h3", "Trade, routes and ports"),
 ("ul", ["Magadha traded textiles, gems, coral, pearls, metals and minerals; salt production was state-regulated; the state kept routes safe and checked adulteration.", "By the 6th century BCE two major land routes: **Dakṣhiṇāpatha** and **Uttarāpatha**, linking inland areas with ports. Ports: **Muziris, Kāveripaṭṭinam (Puhār), Arikameḍu, Masulipaṭnam**; coastal and Indian Ocean sea routes. Trade with Rome expanded in the early centuries CE. The Silappadikāram mentions routes from Kanchipuram to Poompuhar and to Kanyakumari."]),
 ("h3", "Guilds and industry"),
 ("ul", ["**Guilds (śhreṇīs)** were associations of people in the same craft or trade; they rose with the mahājanapadas (6th century BCE). They regulated quality and prices, ran guild courts, and acted as **banks, financiers and trustees**. Jātakas mention 18 guilds. Silver **punch-marked coins** circulated.", "**Nāśhik cave inscription** (2nd century CE, Nahapāna's son-in-law Uśhavadāta): donations were deposited with weavers' guilds at fixed interest to maintain monks' caves.", "Textiles (silk, cotton, wool, linen): hubs Mathurā, Kāśhī, Kāmarūpa. Sanchi's southern gateway records the **ivory workers' guild of Vidiśhā** carving the stone sculptures."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What are the four Vedas?", "Ṛigveda (hymns), Yajurveda (rituals), Sāmaveda (chanted hymns), Atharvaveda (varied hymns, healing)."),
  ("Explain sabhā, samiti and vidhata.", "Vedic assemblies that checked the rājā: sabhā (elite, policy/justice), samiti (large, community), vidhata (popular gathering)."),
  ("What is a janapada? How did it differ from a jana?", "Territorial unit ('where a people set foot'), replacing kin-based identity with land-based identity."),
  ("Why did Magadha become powerful?", "Strategic position, fertile plains and strong rulers."),
  ("Explain the Saptāṅga theory.", "Seven constituents of the state: king, ministers, territory, forts, treasury, army, allies."),
  ("What was chakravarti kśhetra?", "Domain of a universal ruler: land between the Himalayas and the sea."),
  ("Describe administrative divisions in the north and south.", "Bhukti/viṣhaya/paṭṭana/vithi/village in the north; maṇḍalam/koṭṭam/nāḍu/kūṛram/village in the south."),
  ("What was the Kudavolai system?", "Chola village election by drawing palm-leaf names from a pot (Uttaramerur)."),
  ("What is dharma?", "Duty, righteousness and moral conduct that upholds society; not merely religion."),
  ("Distinguish varṇa from jāti.", "Varṇa: four functional categories, flexible. Jāti: later birth-based groups, unlimited in number."),
  ("What were the āśhramas and puruṣhārthas?", "Four life stages and four life goals (dharma, artha, kāma, mokṣha)."),
  ("What do we learn about women in early India?", "Scholars, rishikas, regents (Prabhāvatī Gupta), Sangam workers and poets, donors."),
  ("Who were the Āḻvārs and Nāyanmārs?", "Tamil Bhakti saint-poets devoted to Viṣhṇu (12) and Śhiva (63)."),
  ("Describe the gurukula system.", "Pupils lived with the guru, learned holistic subjects and values, in a disciplined sacred relationship."),
  ("What was the role of guilds?", "Regulated crafts and trade, quality and prices; gave credit and held endowments as banks."),
  ("Why were irrigation works recorded in inscriptions?", "Agriculture was the main revenue source, so irrigation shows state care (Sudarshana Lake, Kallanai)."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Society evolved from kin-based janas to territorial janapadas, mahājanapadas and empires; Magadha led to the Mauryas, followed by the Guptas and Cholas.", "The king protected, gave justice and was advised by a council; assemblies and Saptāṅga limited and structured power.", "Administration ran through provinces, districts and largely autonomous villages (Uttaramerur elections).", "**Dharma** and ethics guided rulers; the pan-Indian ideal of the chakravarti samrāṭ recurs across centuries.", "Varṇa began as functional and flexible; jāti grew later; women, Bhakti and education show a rich, evolving society.", "Agriculture, irrigation, trade routes, ports and guilds powered the economy."])
